e clusters\" — the task explicitly says code points.)\n\nPerformance: hundreds of thousands of characters. O(n) with dict is fine. For extra performance, I could use an array of size 0x110000 for last occurrence, but a dict is fine and simpler. Actually, for a million characters, dict get/set is fine in Python. Let me think about whether to use a list indexed by ord for speed. `last = [-1] * 0x110000` would allocate a ~1.1M-element list — that's ~8.8MB for pointers plus cached small ints, fine but maybe wasteful. A dict is simpler and robust. Let me benchmark both quickly and pick.\n\nActually, let me also consider a \"last occurrence with sentinel\" approach:\n\n```python\ndef longest_unique(text):\n    last = {}\n    start = 0\n    best_start = 0\n    best_len = 0\n    for i, ch in enumerate(text):\n        j = last.get(ch, -1)\n        if j >= start:\n            start = j + 1\n        last[ch] = i\n        length = i - start + 1\n        if length > best_len:\n            best_len = length\n            best_start = start\n    return text[best_start:best_start + best_len]\n```\n\nThis is the standard algorithm. Note that keys in `last` that are before `start` are stale, but checking `j >= start` handles that correctly.\n\nLet me write the final implementation with a brief docstring? The taste says don't add comments unless requested. But a docstring is arguably good production quality. The rules say \"NEVER add comments to code unless explicitly requested by the user\". Hmm, the code_quality section says that. But this is a function in a library; a docstring could help. Let me check taste files first, since the system says to read referenced taste files. The taste.md says \"See [taste/taste.md]\". Let me read that.\n\nAlso, I should check if there are hidden tests or other files. The directory only has task.md, test_public.py, window.py, and .git. There may be hidden grader tests not visible. The public API is `longest_unique`.\n\nLet me read the taste file and check git log to see the baseline.","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"The current implementation is a brute-force O(n³) (even O(n⁴) with `set(s)` inside the nested loop). I need to replace it with a linear-time"}]}}
